BareProxy load experiments: what the 20 applies of the load test stay clear of, 5 October 2026

live/load-experiments.sh runs five cases under load, each on its own BareProxy with two test backends, 1,000 requests per second
over HTTP/1.1 for 24 seconds (the WebSocket case: 400 messages per second on 20 connections for 20 seconds). The applies come
every 1.4 seconds from the 4th second. None of this is part of the load test's pass rule. The machine is the shared 2-CPU VM
described in load-test.log, and the counts differ a little from run to run (a first run of the same cases, before the script
was committed, gave 11 closed WebSocket connections and 17, 0, 24 and 17 failed requests).

case     what each apply does                                         applies    sent  failed  how it fails
drain    removes a backend from the pool that carries WebSocket tunnels   1    8020      11  10 of 20 connections closed when the 3 s drain ended
newpool  adds a new pool and points a route at it, in one apply        10   24000      25  503, No backend is up
twostep  adds the new pool, then 0.6 s later points the route at it    20   24000       0  none
health   changes a pool's health line                                  10   24000      10  503, No backend is up
swap     replaces the only backend of a pool with another one          10   24000      10  503, No backend is up

What they show
  newpool, health and swap: the apply swaps in the new config before the first health check of its new backends has finished. A backend
  that is new to its pool (a new pool, a pool whose health line changed, which counts as new backends, or a replaced backend) is
  'unknown' until that check passes and takes no requests, so a route with no other backend gets 503 for as long as the check takes,
  the first check, which here (a backend on the same machine) took about a millisecond or two: 1 to 2.5 failed requests per apply at
  1,000 requests per second (computed from the counts). With a backend farther away the check takes longer and so does the window.
  Nothing in the output of `plan` or `apply` says so (see run/exp-*/ap.out in a run's folder). The two-step version has no failures.
  drain: a WebSocket connection is a tunnel, and when the pool's drain time ends BareProxy closes the connections of the removed backend,
  in use or not. That is what the README says about drain (connections close after the drain time, 30 seconds by default), and it
  means every apply that removes a backend cuts the WebSocket connections that went through it. The load test's applies leave the
  WebSocket pool alone for that reason.

